# Explicit page context for annotated Markdown: independent design review

Read-only source baseline: 6e7ab06197c412d28305a174a54deda355daa790. Seventeen implementation/spec/manuscript files are fixed in sources.json. No proposed implementation or schema was edited. The existing independently built production CLI was invoked on the actual fixed 01 manuscript with an empty alias list: annotated /2 fails NeedsResolution and creates no output. result.json binds source, executable, command and the prior fixed build record. No future API success, cross-target test or canonical cutover is claimed.

## Recommended first boundary

The immediate 01 link is Relative(path="22-external-extensions.md", fragment=None). Its logical source is doc/spec/01-architecture.md even while its physical Doc input is under doc/migration/authored/. Resolving against the physical directory would be incorrect. Preserve the typed Link and its Ruby label; do not convert it into External or Text.

Use a separately named, explicitly contextual annotated entry point. Retain the existing context-free render/generate refusal unless a versioned public contract deliberately replaces it. The context should bind the current page, all supplied actual Doc documents with their logical Markdown projection paths, aliases/options, and an explicit list of passive Markdown destinations with bounded actual bytes. All input entries must be supplied before resolution. Missing registrations remain errors; do not discover neighboring files from individual link strings or fabricate Doc documents.

For this no-fragment link, PageSet.files already expresses the necessary passive-byte destination. A Markdown-specific adapter can register the exact existing 22.md as PageFile and use pages::resolve without changing the core schema. Require an explicit Markdown destination role/path in this host, rather than treating arbitrary bytes or an .html suffix as rendered Markdown. In the Markdown context its URL destination is the repository Markdown path; it is not the canonical registry's HTML route. A distinct Markdown PageSet can use the Markdown artifact paths as its routes, or an explicit checked mapping can select registration.source/projection paths; either approach must bind the chosen paths and never silently substitute HTML routes.

PageFile has intentionally limited authority: Relative without fragment can resolve to it, LinkTarget.Page cannot, and every Some(fragment) yields FileFragment. Preserve those contracts. An opaque file does not prove a Doc label, emitted anchor, HTML generation or public availability. The actual existing Markdown bytes and host regular-file/path containment checks provide the local file-existence evidence; PageSet resolution itself does no filesystem I/O.

## Existing contracts worth reusing

pages::resolve checks registrations and duplicate/file-directory collisions, structurally encodes all documents with a shared source admission ledger, computes the canonical set digest, checks labels and each document digest, discovers requirements, then resolves links. It normalizes relative components against the logical source and rejects above-root traversal, unsupported path forms and absent entries. Self links require empty path plus a nonempty semantic fragment. All other empty-path cases fail. Page IDs and file destinations have separate meanings and indexes. Foreign/asset/external requirements remain explicit.

CheckedPages privately borrows its exact immutable PageSet and retains per-document digests. A supplied PageLinkPlan is not that proof: portable plan_from_value reexecutes resolve and compares the canonical result. A contextual renderer can consume CheckedPages plus a checked current-page index, but must not accept arbitrary caller maps indexed by bare node ID as proof. Source occurrence keys need both page and node. Validate any node conversion on 32-bit targets. External URI checks, all undisplayed requirements, structure/source/label checks and sticky cancellation remain in the complete operation.

The existing multi-page host uses with_named_input_limits for distinct page IDs and decode namespaces. Repeated calls to the current single-source annotated helper must not accidentally create multiple documents with the same source identity. Reuse the named source path and fresh per-phase budgets, while keeping one cumulative resolver/render output budget. File bytes are passive content bound by the set identity, not replacement SourceSnapshots or an excuse to bypass source admission of Doc documents.

## Semantic fragments and output existence

For actual Doc targets, pages::resolve proves the semantic name exists in that target document. It does not prove the selected Markdown projection emits the corresponding n-UTF8hex anchor. HTML render_pages explicitly checks this second condition via MissingOutputAnchor; the contextual Markdown renderer needs the analogous emitted-anchor inventory. Section IDs, Anchor nodes, unreachable labels and unsupported/hidden constructs are not interchangeable. Do not assume every label returned by labels::check is supported and emitted by annotated /2.

Canonical Doc targets should resolve a semantic fragment to the existing n-hex output convention after verifying the actual target projection's emitted anchors. A compatibility alias is a different mapping; do not hex an already encoded n-name again, or reinterpret a legacy heading slug as a semantic label. Self-page fragments should use the same semantic and emitted-anchor checks. Non-ASCII semantic IDs require exact UTF-8 byte encoding, without Unicode normalization or case folding.

Unmigrated Markdown with a semantic fragment does not fit PageFile's current authority. The minimal first slice should continue rejecting it. Supporting it later needs a separately typed, explicitly supplied mapping from semantic name to actual Markdown-platform anchor, bound to the exact target bytes/renderer evidence. Automatic GitHub slug inference, treating any heading text as a Doc label, or a dummy PageDocument would weaken current contracts. A host-supplied mapping alone proves only the assertion's identity; it does not authenticate that GitHub emitted the anchor. Current spec16/21 separately require real target-platform anchor verification.

## Markdown links and HTML generation remain distinct

canonical::html currently builds a PageSet from canonical registry Doc pages only. If 01 is added while 22 stays unregistered, successful contextual Markdown does not cure HTML MissingPage. The Markdown context must not leak into HTML as a fabricated successful destination. Copying 22.md bytes under a .html route is not HTML rendering.

If this slice must also produce 01 HTML, supply a separate explicit HTML dependency graph using actual reviewed Doc inputs, their logical source names and routes, and run the existing complete page generator. A dependency Doc can be a declared build input without declaring its Markdown source migrated, but this requires its own content/identity/output evidence and must not be conflated with canonical ownership registration. Otherwise keep the HTML limitation visible and do not call that part of 01 cutover complete. No unregistered Markdown page's public HTML generation is currently established by this design.

## Identity, ordering, resource and write boundaries

Generated Markdown metadata must bind the contextual mode/version, the current logical projection path and current page identity, ordered context/set identity, target-selection paths and exact aliases/options. Source digest plus current alias digest alone is insufficient when a context can change destinations. Keep old schema domains unchanged if the core PageSet schema is reused; introduce a distinct host context contract and renderer identifier for the new capability. Strict JSON must reject unknown and duplicate fields at every relevant nesting depth.

Avoid identity recursion: for Doc entries bind source/Document values and aliases, not their generated Markdown bytes that themselves contain the context digest. Passive, unmigrated Markdown bytes can be included as file content. Resolve the entire finite graph once and render pages without recursively invoking generation on link targets; valid A-to-B-to-A and self links must not create cycles in the implementation's call stack or metadata hashes.

Perform budget charges before path/index/string/URL allocation and traversal; apply existing isize capacity bounds. All context validation, set hashing, lookup, anchor inventory and output work shares the caller's finite budget. Do not reset a stopped budget to regenerate target anchors or retry a legacy path. Existing partial-writer output must remain private on any error. Host commands should finish validation/generation before create_new writes and must retain the existing explicit partial-I/O-failure limitation.

URL serialization needs two distinct steps: derive a relative destination from validated artifact paths, then URL-encode as needed, then escape for Markdown. Do not feed a full serialized HTML href through a regex to obtain its URL, append unchecked fragment text, decode percent escapes into path traversal or normalize &amp; twice. Preserve current nested-Link/Break refusal and code-adjacency checks through Ruby/Anno/Concat, lists and table cells. GitHub resolves a relative link from the file containing it, so the generated Markdown location is the base, not physical Doc input or HTML output. CommonMark allows escaped punctuation and angle-enclosed destinations but does not validate repository existence or semantic anchors. These observations are supported by [GitHub relative-link documentation](https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/writing-on-github/getting-started-with-writing-and-formatting-on-github/basic-writing-and-formatting-syntax#relative-links) and [CommonMark links](https://spec.commonmark.org/0.31.2/#links).

## Concrete acceptance cases for the root implementation

| Case | Required observation |
| --- | --- |
| Actual 01, explicit passive 22.md, no fragment | Parsed label annotations and link retained; decoded destination is 22-external-extensions.md from doc/spec/01-architecture.md |
| Same bytes, physical input moved to scratch/authored | Same semantic destination; physical metadata differences only where defined |
| File exists nearby but omitted from context | Missing target; no filesystem discovery success |
| Registered target missing, symlink/junction outside root, directory, duplicate case-folded host path or oversized bytes | Host fails before output, preserving originals |
| Same link string from two different logical directories | Resolves against each current page's logical source; cannot reuse another page's answer |
| ../ within root versus excess ../, empty segment, absolute/network path, percent traversal, backslash, query/fragment embedded in path | Correct normalization or rejection according to existing path contract |
| Passive Markdown with Some(fragment), including empty fragment, or LinkTarget.Page naming passive file | Existing FileFragment/MissingPage contract retained |
| Canonical Doc target with valid/missing semantic Section, Anchor-only unsupported output, omitted target projection | Real semantic and emitted-output checks; never fabricate destination ID |
| Japanese semantic ID, existing n-name, legacy alias, percent literal | Exact namespace/encoding; no slug guess, double hex or double decoding |
| Relative empty path with valid self fragment; empty path without fragment | First resolves with emitted-anchor proof, second rejects |
| Two pages using the same node IDs, same label names, distinct sources | Page ownership remains distinct; cross-page map cannot select wrong occurrence |
| Same source/revision but differing content across Doc inputs | Existing shared-admission rejection retained |
| A links B and B links A | Finite one-pass preparation succeeds if targets are otherwise representable |
| Target bytes, route/projection, aliases, order or current page changed after recorded metadata | Stale context/result rejects; unchanged repeated run deterministic |
| Fresh CBOR PageSet followed by resolve, altered raw plan | Real receiver execution succeeds only for valid closure; spoofed plan cannot confer authority |
| Relative links inside Ruby/Anno/Strong, code labels and table cells with punctuation | Independent CommonMark/GFM reparse preserves label/text/code and decoded URL |
| Nested link, Break in label, adjacent code hidden by link/decorations | Existing refusal preserved |
| Unused target contains invalid source/label; hidden branch contains asset/foreign/invalid URI | Whole admitted context validation/requirement checks not bypassed for used link convenience |
| Success Usage-1 per applicable resource, caller consumed Work, caller depth, cancellation and retry | Typed sticky stop, no output, original documents/context unchanged |
| Canonical Markdown passes but target absent from HTML PageSet | HTML explicitly fails; no claim that passive .md was generated as HTML |

These are proposed acceptance inputs, not executed tests of an unimplemented API. Only the current real 01 /2 refusal was independently executed for this design record.
